Patch: relativeSkip
Letter: I
Requires: none
Files: src/datetime.js
Summary: two calls toRelative makes that cannot affect its answer

I: two calls toRelative makes that cannot affect its answer

  toRelative    padding    the padding option is rarely passed, and when it is
                           not, padding is 0 and the method still calls
                           this.plus(0) — a clone whose offset is re-derived to
                           produce a copy of the receiver.
  diffRelative  unit loop  the loop asks diff() for years, then months, then
                           days, and so on until one comes back at 1 or more, so
                           a "3 months ago" answer pays for a full calendar-year
                           diff first. A unit cannot reach 1 unless the two
                           instants are at least one of it apart, and the
                           shortest each unit can be in local time is a
                           constant, so the diffs that can only return 0 are
                           skippable without being run.

The floors are the shortest that unit can be anywhere, rounded down again: a
month is 28 days in February, a day is 23 hours where the clocks go forward, a
quarter is 90. The margin is not there because the number is uncertain; it is
there so that a zone with a rule nobody here anticipated still lands above the
floor rather than under it.

Two things bound this, both of which the tests turn on:

  * The skip is off for the calendary path. toRelativeCalendar counts boundary
    crossings rather than elapsed time, where 23:00 and 01:00 are two hours
    apart and one day apart, so no spread implies anything about its answer.
  * A unit the table does not name has no floor and is always asked, which is
    what keeps an unknown one throwing InvalidUnitError where it threw before.
    The table is null-prototype for that: a numeric "floor" reaching it by
    inheritance would skip the diff that was going to throw and answer instead.

What makes this worth more than the calls it removes is what those calls were
doing to E. Each plus() walks E's transition-free interval cache, which has two
slots, so the unnecessary lookups were evicting the working set and making the
necessary ones miss as well. Measured on the complete build, fastest of three,
per 20,000: a base 90 days out goes 749.6ms to 79.0ms and 15.2 ICU calls per
value to zero, one three hours out 241.4 to 57.3 at zero either way, one two
years out 432.5 to 61.7 and 9.0 calls to zero. The corollary is that this is
worth little without E, and E is worth more once this is in.

That is also why it is a patch rather than two lines folded into H. Removing it
alone from the complete tree costs 7.80x on node and 3.93x on bun on the
toRelative column, against a control — the same tree built twice and timed
against itself — that reaches 1.35x and 1.42x. H, which is under the same
method through adjustTime, costs 1.70x there. The two are on the same column for
different reasons and at different sizes, and only one of them is on any other
column.

Verified against stock over 194,400 calls: six zones including Lord Howe's
half-hour DST and Chatham's 45-minute offset, both toRelative and
toRelativeCalendar, spreads either side of every floor and of each unit's true
minimum, both signs, and the round, rounding, unit, array-unit and padding
options. Zero differ. The sweep is in
benchmarks/test/relative-skip-patch.test.ts.

